The security example can be improved by adding robust input validation and demonstrating the session ownership check. When validating objects from an external API (like a request body), use explicit checks like 'obj != null && typeof obj === "object"' instead of truthiness or optional chaining to be more robust. Also, verifying that the user owns the session (:sid) is a critical security step. Finally, using sequential await in a loop is a performance 'gotcha' that could be optimized with Promise.all.

```suggestion
app.post('/api/sessions/:sid/tracks', async (req, res) => {
  const isValidBody = req.body != null && typeof req.body === 'object';
  if (!isValidBody || !Array.isArray(req.body.tracks)) return res.status(400).json({error: 'Invalid tracks'});
  if (!await canAccessSession(req.user.id, req.params.sid)) return res.status(403).json({error: 'Unauth'});
  for (const t of req.body.tracks)
    if (!await canAccessTrack(req.user.id, t.trackName)) return res.status(403).json({error: 'Unauth'});
  // CF API call
});
```

